{# ===== LAYER 1 ===== #}
<section class="chapter" aria-label="Process Analytics" id="process-analytics">
  <div class="chapter__inner">

    <div class="kicker"><span class="kdot"></span>Layer 01</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Workflow Intelligence — <span class="accent">Process Analytics</span></h2>
    <p class="muted">The operational layer. Measures how work actually moves — where it accelerates, where it stalls, and what structural patterns predict outcomes before they happen.</p>

    <p>Every stage transition, scheduling delay, and pass-through rate leaves a measurable trace. Process analytics turns that trace into a real-time picture of pipeline health that teams can act on — not just report on.</p>

    <div class="grid3">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Pipeline Velocity</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Time in stage by role and team</li>
          <li>Stage-to-stage transition rates</li>
          <li>Scheduling lag and reschedule rates</li>
          <li>Pipeline age distribution</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Funnel Health</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Pass-through rates by stage</li>
          <li>Drop-off patterns by source</li>
          <li>Offer acceptance rates</li>
          <li>Decline and withdrawal reasons</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Capacity</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Requisition load per recruiter</li>
          <li>Interview panel utilization</li>
          <li>Open req aging</li>
          <li>Throughput vs. headcount plan</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">Workflow intelligence is the most established layer — most recruiting teams already track some version of it.</div>

    <div class="ctaRow ctaRow--left" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
      <a class="deepLink" href="/tools/metrics/">Metrics A[i]gent →</a>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

{# ===== LAYER 2 ===== #}
<section class="chapter" aria-label="Candidate Capability Analytics" id="candidate-analytics">
  <div class="chapter__inner">

    <div class="kicker"><span class="kdot"></span>Layer 02</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Behavioral Intelligence — <span class="accent">Performance Analytics</span></h2>
    <p class="muted">The people layer. Measures what candidate behavior during the hiring process reveals about capability — before a single interview score is submitted.</p>

    <p>Behavioral economics research shows that how people engage with a process is itself a signal. Responsiveness, follow-through, communication quality, and attention to detail during hiring predict the same patterns on the job.</p>

    <div class="grid3">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Engagement</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Response time to outreach</li>
          <li>Scheduling follow-through</li>
          <li>Reschedule and no-show rates</li>
          <li>Application completeness</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Communication</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Clarity in written responses</li>
          <li>Preparation signals in interviews</li>
          <li>Question quality</li>
          <li>Attention to stated requirements</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Consistency</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Alignment across resume, application, and interview</li>
          <li>Follow-through on next steps</li>
          <li>Interest signal reliability</li>
          <li>Pattern vs. one-off behavior</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">Behavioral intelligence is standard in fields like organizational psychology, sales, and UX research — but almost never applied systematically to hiring. The signals exist. Most teams just aren't measuring them.</div>

    <div class="ctaRow ctaRow--left" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
      <a class="deepLink" href="/articles/decision-science-metrics/">Related article →</a>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

{# ===== LAYER 3 ===== #}
<section class="chapter" aria-label="Decision Intelligence" id="decision-intelligence">
  <div class="chapter__inner">

    <div class="kicker"><span class="kdot"></span>Layer 03</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Decision Intelligence — <span class="accent">System Analytics</span></h2>
    <p class="muted">The judgment layer. Measures how reliably internal hiring decisions are made — and where inconsistency, bias, or variance is degrading quality without anyone noticing.</p>

    <p>Hiring decisions are made by humans under pressure, with incomplete information, and without a feedback loop. Decision science applies structured measurement to that judgment layer — making variance visible, patterns diagnosable, and improvement possible.</p>

    <div class="grid3">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Interviewer Calibration</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Scoring variance by interviewer</li>
          <li>Rubric adherence rates</li>
          <li>Score distribution patterns</li>
          <li>Halo and recency effect indicators</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Decision Consistency</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Panel agreement rates</li>
          <li>Override frequency and direction</li>
          <li>Debrief-to-decision alignment</li>
          <li>Decision speed vs. accuracy</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Outcome Feedback</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Hire quality correlation</li>
          <li>Early attrition by group</li>
          <li>Offer decline patterns</li>
          <li>Closed-loop signal refinement</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">The same concepts used to evaluate LLM reliability — consistency, calibration, and variance across outputs — apply directly to human hiring decisions. Few organizations have ever measured their judgment layer systematically.</div>

    <div class="ctaRow ctaRow--left" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
      <a class="deepLink" href="/articles/interviewer-scoring-style/">Interviewer Scoring article →</a>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

